{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794589598805,"sessionID":"ses_e2a20a4d7ffeIt6OmrD13sAn8e","part":{"id":"prt_1d5e02455001L473182A9Eykhv","sessionID":"ses_e2a20a4d7ffeIt6OmrD13sAn8e","messageID":"msg_1d5e0136f001w1sghcY0N7qrBe","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589600312,"sessionID":"ses_e2a20a4d7ffeIt6OmrD13sAn8e","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5e028b4001FOUc0gxNbShIrv","sessionID":"ses_e2a20a4d7ffeIt6OmrD13sAn8e","messageID":"msg_1d5e0136f001w1sghcY0N7qrBe","type":"tool","id":"call_70b80cd854db43c5a640dd88","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589599924,"end":1794589600312}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589600331,"sessionID":"ses_e2a20a4d7ffeIt6OmrD13sAn8e","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5e026b0001E1fbplu3pWjvhV","sessionID":"ses_e2a20a4d7ffeIt6OmrD13sAn8e","messageID":"msg_1d5e0136f001w1sghcY0N7qrBe","type":"tool","id":"call_a2f1332b45c64e418a4bc18c","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/readme/writing-patterns.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/readme/writing-patterns.md, lines 1-238\n1: ---\n2: title: README Writing Patterns\n3: description: Per-section writing tips, heading hierarchy, analogy patterns, recommended table patterns and code-block conventions for READMEs.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"readme writing patterns\"\n6:   - \"readme section standards\"\n7:   - \"readme table patterns\"\n8:   - \"readme analogy patterns\"\n9:   - \"readme heading hierarchy\"\n10: importance_tier: normal\n11: contextType: implementation\n12: version: 1.2.2.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # README Writing Patterns\n16: \n17: Open this while drafting a single section, once the section list is settled and the question is how to shape that section. It is overflow detail behind `SKILL.md` Sections 5 and 6 (output shapes), which decide which sections to include.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### Purpose\n24: \n25: These are the writing patterns that make a README scannable and consistent: what each section is for, how headings nest, when an analogy helps, and which table shapes carry which content.\n26: \n27: ### When to Use\n28: \n29: - Drafting a specific README section and wanting a writing tip\n30: - Choosing a table pattern for comparison, statistics or parameters\n31: - Confirming heading, code-block or placeholder conventions\n32: \n33: ### Core Principle\n34: \n35: Show it in a table when the content is comparable, and lead every section with what the reader needs first.\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 2. SECTION WRITING STANDARDS\n40: \n41: Workflow guidance for the sections you choose to include. The fillable scaffold is [readme-template.md](../../assets/readme-template.md) Section 6.\n42: \n43: ### Overview\n44: \n45: Establish what this is, why it exists and key metrics. The comparison table is the most persuasive element: lead with the user's problem, then how this solves it. Show concrete differences, not marketing claims.\n46: \n47: ### Quick Start\n48: \n49: Get users to a working state in under 2 minutes. \"30-Second Setup\" is aspirational, so aim for it. Assume nothing is installed, or state prerequisites clearly.\n50: \n51: ### Features\n52: \n53: Comprehensive feature documentation with two-tier structure.\n54: \n55: - Split into a narrative subsection (`### 3.1 HOW IT WORKS`) and a reference subsection (`### 3.2 TECHNICAL REFERENCE`).\n56: - Use numbered H3 ALL CAPS subsections with `---` dividers between them, and numbered H4 ALL CAPS sub-subsections (3.1.1, 3.1.2) within narrative subsections.\n57: - Open each sub-subsection with 1-2 plain-language sentences, and bold key term names on first use.\n58: \n59: Name the feature in its heading and lead with its benefit: `### Spec Kit: a written record of every change` rather than `### Powered by Spec Kit`. When a document covers four or more major features, give each one its own numbered H2 section instead of splitting a single Features section, so every feature gets the full two-tier treatment and its own anchor.\n60: \n61: ### Structure\n62: \n63: Help users navigate the project. Use an ASCII tree with purpose annotations, 2-3 levels deep.\n64: \n65: ### Configuration\n66: \n67: Complete configuration reference. Show a complete example config, not fragments, and explain the \"why\" not the \"what\".\n68: \n69: ### Usage Examples\n70: \n71: Real-world usage patterns users can copy. Build complexity progressively from basic to advanced.\n72: \n73: ### Troubleshooting\n74: \n75: Self-service problem resolution. Lead with what the user sees in their own words, not internal error names. Use \"What you see / Common causes / Fix\" format, separate issues with `---` dividers and keep each issue self-contained.\n76: \n77: ### FAQ\n78: \n79: Answer frequently asked questions. Bold **Q:** with A: format, 2-3 sentence answers.\n80: \n81: ### Related Documents\n82: \n83: Guide users to additional resources. Use relative paths for internal docs.\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. STRUCTURAL FORMAT RULES\n88: \n89: ### Heading Hierarchy\n90: \n91: | Level | Format | Example |\n92: |-------|--------|---------|\n93: | **H1** | Plain title, no number | `# Project Name` |\n94: | **H2** | Numbered, ALL CAPS | `## 1. OVERVIEW` |\n95: | **H3 (numbered)** | Numbered, ALL CAPS | `### 3.1 TOPIC NAME` |\n96: | **H3 (unnumbered)** | Title Case | `### Configuration` |\n97: | **H4 (numbered)** | Numbered, ALL CAPS | `#### 3.1.1 SUBTOPIC` |\n98: | **H4 (unnumbered)** | Title Case | `#### Options` |\n99: \n100: Numbered subsections appear inside Feature sections (3.1, 3.2 at H3 and 3.1.1, 3.1.2 at H4). Unnumbered subsections appear everywhere else.\n101: \n102: ### Emoji Use\n103: \n104: Two registers apply. The repository front-page README may use decorative emoji on its numbered H2 headings, the way `## 5. 📋 SPEC KIT` names and signals a feature. Every other README stays on the technical register: semantic emoji only (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) for rules and status markers, and only where the symbol carries the meaning. Never copy the front-page README's decorative style into a skill, feature, component or code-folder README.\n105: \n106: ### Paragraphs and Bullets\n107: \n108: Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences, about 10-15 words each and about 60 words a paragraph. Use bullets only for items a reader scans or compares: commands, options, steps and parallel facts. A run of bullets that each hold a sentence of explanation reads better as a paragraph. Balance the two: a section that is all bullets reads like notes, and one that is all prose hides what a reader scans for. Word counts guide rather than gate, so more short sentences are always fine.\n109: \n110: ### Blockquote Tagline\n111: \n112: Place a one-sentence description in a blockquote immediately after the H1 title, before the first `---`.\n113: \n114: ```markdown\n115: # Skill Advisor - MCP Server\n116: \n117: > Routes every prompt to the skill that owns it, so the right instructions load before the first tool call.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: ```\n121: \n122: Do not add a Table of Contents and do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments. READMEs rely on the numbered H2 hierarchy for navigation. Use `---` horizontal rules between H2 sections.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. ANALOGY PATTERNS\n127: \n128: Analogies make technical concepts stick. Use them in narrative sections of Project, Skill and Feature READMEs. Do not use analogies in Component or Code Folder READMEs.\n129: \n130: | Analogy | Good For |\n131: |---------|----------|\n132: | **Librarian** | Search, retrieval, filing, organization |\n133: | **Filing cabinet** | Storage, folders, hierarchy |\n134: | **Bouncer at the door** | Quality gates, validation, rejection |\n135: | **Save point in a video game** | Checkpoints, snapshots, rollback |\n136: | **Triage nurse** | Routing, classification, priority |\n137: | **Assembly line** | Pipelines, stages, sequential processing |\n138: \n139: **Rules**: One analogy per concept. Place it after the technical statement, not before. Drop the analogy if the concept is already clear from plain language. Maximum 2 \"Think of it as/like\" per document.\n140: \n141: ---\n142: \n143: ## 5. TABLE PATTERNS\n144: \n145: Tables are scannable. Use them for feature comparisons, configuration options, file listings, requirements and quick-reference data.\n146: \n147: **\"How This Compares\"** shows capability comparison. Place it in Overview.\n148: \n149: ```markdown\n150: | Capability | Without [Project] | With [Project] |\n151: |------------|-------------------|----------------|\n152: | Documentation | Ad hoc, inconsistent | Templated at 4 levels |\n153: | Search | Ctrl+F in files | 5-channel hybrid search |\n154: ```\n155: \n156: **Key Statistics** shows metrics at a glance. Place it in Overview after the description.\n157: \n158: ```markdown\n159: | Category | Count | Details |\n160: |----------|-------|---------|\n161: | Spec Memory tools | 39 | MCP registration and full-parity daemon-backed CLI |\n162: | Commands | 14 | 8 spec_kit + 6 memory |\n163: ```\n164: \n165: **Key Features** summarizes capabilities. Place it in Overview.\n166: \n167: ```markdown\n168: | Feature | What It Does |\n169: |---------|-------------|\n170: | **Spec Folder Workflow** | Creates documentation for every file-modifying conversation |\n171: | **Hybrid Search** | Checks 5 sources at once and fuses results |\n172: ```\n173: \n174: **Before/After** shows the contrast. Use it in Overview or Features.\n175: \n176: ```markdown\n177: | Without [Project] | With [Project] |\n178: |--------------------|----------------|\n179: | Manual config for each file | Zero-config with smart defaults |\n180: | Errors discovered in production | Caught at build time |\n181: ```\n182: \n183: **Parameter** is a reference-tier table. Use it in Features reference subsections and Configuration.\n184: \n185: ```markdown\n186: | Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n187: |-----------|------|---------|-------------|\n188: | `query` | string | required | Free-text search query |\n189: | `limit` | number | `10` | Results to return (1-100) |\n190: ```\n191: \n192: **Itemized Inventory** lists named artifacts such as templates, scripts, commands and config files where each item needs more than a table cell. Use a bold code name with short bullets saying what the item is and when it applies.\n193: \n194: ```markdown\n195: **`spec.md`**\n196: - What the feature is and why it exists.\n197: - Required at every level.\n198: \n199: **`decision-record.md`**\n200: - Architecture decision records: choices, alternatives and consequences.\n201: - Required backing for any `Waived` or `Superseded` acceptance-criteria row.\n202: ```\n203: \n204: Group related items under an unnumbered `#####` heading when the inventory has natural groups, for example by how each item is triggered. Prefer the Key Files or Parameter table instead when every item fits one line.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 6. CODE-BLOCK AND PLACEHOLDER CONVENTIONS\n209: \n210: Always specify language for syntax highlighting, and show expected output for verification commands.\n211: \n212: ```bash\n213: # Install dependencies\n214: npm install\n215: \n216: npm --version\n217: # Expected: 10.2.0 or higher\n218: ```\n219: \n220: Use `[PLACEHOLDER]` format with descriptive names:\n221: \n222: | Placeholder | Purpose |\n223: |-------------|---------|\n224: | `[PROJECT_NAME]` | Name of the project |\n225: | `[DESCRIPTION]` | Brief description |\n226: | `[COMMAND]` | Actual command to run |\n227: | `[VERSION]` | Version number |\n228: | `[PATH]` | File or directory path |\n229: \n230: ---\n231: \n232: ## 7. CROSS-REFERENCES\n233: \n234: - [types-and-voice.md](types-and-voice.md) - README types, progressive disclosure and two-tier voice\n235: - [quality-and-checklist.md](quality-and-checklist.md) - Quality criteria and pre-publish checklist\n236: - [readme-template.md](../../assets/readme-template.md) - Fillable general README scaffold\n237: - [readme-code-template.md](../../assets/readme-code-template.md) - Code-folder README scaffold\n238: - [core-standards.md](../../../shared/references/core-standards.md) - Document formatting rules","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/readme/writing-patterns.md, lines 1-238\n1: ---\n2: title: README Writing Patterns\n3: description: Per-section writing tips, heading hierarchy, analogy patterns, recommended table patterns and code-block conventions for READMEs.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"readme writing patterns\"\n6:   - \"readme section standards\"\n7:   - \"readme table patterns\"\n8:   - \"readme analogy patterns\"\n9:   - \"readme heading hierarchy\"\n10: importance_tier: normal\n11: contextType: implementation\n12: version: 1.2.2.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # README Writing Patterns\n16: \n17: Open this while drafting a single section, once the section list is settled and the question is how to shape that section. It is overflow detail behind `SKILL.md` Sections 5 and 6 (output shapes), which decide which sections to include.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### Purpose\n24: \n25: These are the writing patterns that make a README scannable and consistent: what each section is for, how headings nest, when an analogy helps, and which table shapes carry which content.\n26: \n27: ### When to Use\n28: \n29: - Drafting a specific README section and wanting a writing tip\n30: - Choosing a table pattern for comparison, statistics or parameters\n31: - Confirming heading, code-block or placeholder conventions\n32: \n33: ### Core Principle\n34: \n35: Show it in a table when the content is comparable, and lead every section with what the reader needs first.\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 2. SECTION WRITING STANDARDS\n40: \n41: Workflow guidance for the sections you choose to include. The fillable scaffold is [readme-template.md](../../assets/readme-template.md) Section 6.\n42: \n43: ### Overview\n44: \n45: Establish what this is, why it exists and key metrics. The comparison table is the most persuasive element: lead with the user's problem, then how this solves it. Show concrete differences, not marketing claims.\n46: \n47: ### Quick Start\n48: \n49: Get users to a working state in under 2 minutes. \"30-Second Setup\" is aspirational, so aim for it. Assume nothing is installed, or state prerequisites clearly.\n50: \n51: ### Features\n52: \n53: Comprehensive feature documentation with two-tier structure.\n54: \n55: - Split into a narrative subsection (`### 3.1 HOW IT WORKS`) and a reference subsection (`### 3.2 TECHNICAL REFERENCE`).\n56: - Use numbered H3 ALL CAPS subsections with `---` dividers between them, and numbered H4 ALL CAPS sub-subsections (3.1.1, 3.1.2) within narrative subsections.\n57: - Open each sub-subsection with 1-2 plain-language sentences, and bold key term names on first use.\n58: \n59: Name the feature in its heading and lead with its benefit: `### Spec Kit: a written record of every change` rather than `### Powered by Spec Kit`. When a document covers four or more major features, give each one its own numbered H2 section instead of splitting a single Features section, so every feature gets the full two-tier treatment and its own anchor.\n60: \n61: ### Structure\n62: \n63: Help users navigate the project. Use an ASCII tree with purpose annotations, 2-3 levels deep.\n64: \n65: ### Configuration\n66: \n67: Complete configuration reference. Show a complete example config, not fragments, and explain the \"why\" not the \"what\".\n68: \n69: ### Usage Examples\n70: \n71: Real-world usage patterns users can copy. Build complexity progressively from basic to advanced.\n72: \n73: ### Troubleshooting\n74: \n75: Self-service problem resolution. Lead with what the user sees in their own words, not internal error names. Use \"What you see / Common causes / Fix\" format, separate issues with `---` dividers and keep each issue self-contained.\n76: \n77: ### FAQ\n78: \n79: Answer frequently asked questions. Bold **Q:** with A: format, 2-3 sentence answers.\n80: \n81: ### Related Documents\n82: \n83: Guide users to additional resources. Use relative paths for internal docs.\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. STRUCTURAL FORMAT RULES\n88: \n89: ### Heading Hierarchy\n90: \n91: | Level | Format | Example |\n92: |-------|--------|---------|\n93: | **H1** | Plain title, no number | `# Project Name` |\n94: | **H2** | Numbered, ALL CAPS | `## 1. OVERVIEW` |\n95: | **H3 (numbered)** | Numbered, ALL CAPS | `### 3.1 TOPIC NAME` |\n96: | **H3 (unnumbered)** | Title Case | `### Configuration` |\n97: | **H4 (numbered)** | Numbered, ALL CAPS | `#### 3.1.1 SUBTOPIC` |\n98: | **H4 (unnumbered)** | Title Case | `#### Options` |\n99: \n100: Numbered subsections appear inside Feature sections (3.1, 3.2 at H3 and 3.1.1, 3.1.2 at H4). Unnumbered subsections appear everywhere else.\n101: \n102: ### Emoji Use\n103: \n104: Two registers apply. The repository front-page README may use decorative emoji on its numbered H2 headings, the way `## 5. 📋 SPEC KIT` names and signals a feature. Every other README stays on the technical register: semantic emoji only (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) for rules and status markers, and only where the symbol carries the meaning. Never copy the front-page README's decorative style into a skill, feature, component or code-folder README.\n105: \n106: ### Paragraphs and Bullets\n107: \n108: Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences, about 10-15 words each and about 60 words a paragraph. Use bullets only for items a reader scans or compares: commands, options, steps and parallel facts. A run of bullets that each hold a sentence of explanation reads better as a paragraph. Balance the two: a section that is all bullets reads like notes, and one that is all prose hides what a reader scans for. Word counts guide rather than gate, so more short sentences are always fine.\n109: \n110: ### Blockquote Tagline\n111: \n112: Place a one-sentence description in a blockquote immediately after the H1 title, before the first `---`.\n113: \n114: ```markdown\n115: # Skill Advisor - MCP Server\n116: \n117: > Routes every prompt to the skill that owns it, so the right instructions load before the first tool call.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: ```\n121: \n122: Do not add a Table of Contents and do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments. READMEs rely on the numbered H2 hierarchy for navigation. Use `---` horizontal rules between H2 sections.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. ANALOGY PATTERNS\n127: \n128: Analogies make technical concepts stick. Use them in narrative sections of Project, Skill and Feature READMEs. Do not use analogies in Component or Code Folder READMEs.\n129: \n130: | Analogy | Good For |\n131: |---------|----------|\n132: | **Librarian** | Search, retrieval, filing, organization |\n133: | **Filing cabinet** | Storage, folders, hierarchy |\n134: | **Bouncer at the door** | Quality gates, validation, rejection |\n135: | **Save point in a video game** | Checkpoints, snapshots, rollback |\n136: | **Triage nurse** | Routing, classification, priority |\n137: | **Assembly line** | Pipelines, stages, sequential processing |\n138: \n139: **Rules**: One analogy per concept. Place it after the technical statement, not before. Drop the analogy if the concept is already clear from plain language. Maximum 2 \"Think of it as/like\" per document.\n140: \n141: ---\n142: \n143: ## 5. TABLE PATTERNS\n144: \n145: Tables are scannable. Use them for feature comparisons, configuration options, file listings, requirements and quick-reference data.\n146: \n147: **\"How This Compares\"** shows capability comparison. Place it in Overview.\n148: \n149: ```markdown\n150: | Capability | Without [Project] | With [Project] |\n151: |------------|-------------------|----------------|\n152: | Documentation | Ad hoc, inconsistent | Templated at 4 levels |\n153: | Search | Ctrl+F in files | 5-channel hybrid search |\n154: ```\n155: \n156: **Key Statistics** shows metrics at a glance. Place it in Overview after the description.\n157: \n158: ```markdown\n159: | Category | Count | Details |\n160: |----------|-------|---------|\n161: | Spec Memory tools | 39 | MCP registration and full-parity daemon-backed CLI |\n162: | Commands | 14 | 8 spec_kit + 6 memory |\n163: ```\n164: \n165: **Key Features** summarizes capabilities. Place it in Overview.\n166: \n167: ```markdown\n168: | Feature | What It Does |\n169: |---------|-------------|\n170: | **Spec Folder Workflow** | Creates documentation for every file-modifying conversation |\n171: | **Hybrid Search** | Checks 5 sources at once and fuses results |\n172: ```\n173: \n174: **Before/After** shows the contrast. Use it in Overview or Features.\n175: \n176: ```markdown\n177: | Without [Project] | With [Project] |\n178: |--------------------|----------------|\n179: | Manual config for each file | Zero-config with smart defaults |\n180: | Errors discovered in production | Caught at build time |\n181: ```\n182: \n183: **Parameter** is a reference-tier table. Use it in Features reference subsections and Configuration.\n184: \n185: ```markdown\n186: | Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n187: |-----------|------|---------|-------------|\n188: | `query` | string | required | Free-text search query |\n189: | `limit` | number | `10` | Results to return (1-100) |\n190: ```\n191: \n192: **Itemized Inventory** lists named artifacts such as templates, scripts, commands and config files where each item needs more than a table cell. Use a bold code name with short bullets saying what the item is and when it applies.\n193: \n194: ```markdown\n195: **`spec.md`**\n196: - What the feature is and why it exists.\n197: - Required at every level.\n198: \n199: **`decision-record.md`**\n200: - Architecture decision records: choices, alternatives and consequences.\n201: - Required backing for any `Waived` or `Superseded` acceptance-criteria row.\n202: ```\n203: \n204: Group related items under an unnumbered `#####` heading when the inventory has natural groups, for example by how each item is triggered. Prefer the Key Files or Parameter table instead when every item fits one line.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 6. CODE-BLOCK AND PLACEHOLDER CONVENTIONS\n209: \n210: Always specify language for syntax highlighting, and show expected output for verification commands.\n211: \n212: ```bash\n213: # Install dependencies\n214: npm install\n215: \n216: npm --version\n217: # Expected: 10.2.0 or higher\n218: ```\n219: \n220: Use `[PLACEHOLDER]` format with descriptive names:\n221: \n222: | Placeholder | Purpose |\n223: |-------------|---------|\n224: | `[PROJECT_NAME]` | Name of the project |\n225: | `[DESCRIPTION]` | Brief description |\n226: | `[COMMAND]` | Actual command to run |\n227: | `[VERSION]` | Version number |\n228: | `[PATH]` | File or directory path |\n229: \n230: ---\n231: \n232: ## 7. CROSS-REFERENCES\n233: \n234: - [types-and-voice.md](types-and-voice.md) - README types, progressive disclosure and two-tier voice\n235: - [quality-and-checklist.md](quality-and-checklist.md) - Quality criteria and pre-publish checklist\n236: - [readme-template.md](../../assets/readme-template.md) - Fillable general README scaffold\n237: - [readme-code-template.md](../../assets/readme-code-template.md) - Code-folder README scaffold\n238: - [core-standards.md](../../../shared/references/core-standards.md) - Document formatting rules"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/readme/writing-patterns.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589599408,"end":1794589600331}}}}
